-- title:
--   The kernel ideal of ρ is an mathcal O_D-stable subgroup
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $B$ a commutative ring, and let $\Phi$, $Y$ be formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $B$ of the project's kind: each consists of a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $B$, a map $a \mapsto \mathrm{act}(a)$ from $\mathbb Z_{p^2} := W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ to pairs of power series in two variables, and a further pair $\varpi$, all of $\mathrm{act}(a)$ and $\varpi$ being endomorphisms of the law $F$ (no constant term, and compatible with $F$ in the usual two-variable sense), with $\mathrm{act}(1)$ the identity, $\mathrm{act}$ multiplicative and additive for $F$, $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}(p)$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Witt vector Frobenius $\sigma$. Let $\rho, \beta$ be pairs of power series in two variables over $B$ such that $\rho$ is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism $\Phi \to Y$ and $\beta$ one $Y \to \Phi$, that is, each is a homomorphism of the underlying laws and commutes with the $\mathrm{act}(a)$ and with $\varpi$. Let $N \in \mathbb N$ and assume $\beta \circ \rho = \mathrm{act}_\Phi(p^N)$, i.e. substituting $\rho$ into each component of $\beta$ gives the corresponding component of $[p^N]$ on $\Phi$. Write $J = (\rho_1, \rho_2)$ for the ideal of $B[\![x_1,x_2]\!]$ generated by the two components of $\rho$. Then four statements hold: the ideal generated by the components of $\mathrm{act}_\Phi(p^N)$ is contained in $J$; for every $a \in \mathbb Z_{p^2}$ and every $G \in J$, the substitution of $\mathrm{act}_\Phi(a)$ into $G$ lies in $J$; for every $G \in J$, the substitution of $\varpi_\Phi$ into $G$ lies in $J$; and for every $G \in J$, the substitution of the group law $F_\Phi$ into $G$, an element of $B[\![x_1,x_2,y_1,y_2]\!]$, lies in the ideal generated by the images of $\rho_1, \rho_2$ under the two inclusions sending the variables to the first, respectively the second, group of variables.
--
--   In classical terms: if $[p^N]$ on $\Phi$ factors through an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism $\rho$, then the kernel ideal of $\rho$ cuts out a closed subgroup of $\Phi[p^N]$ stable under the action of $\mathcal O_D = \mathbb Z_{p^2}[\varpi]$, here expressed entirely through ideals of power series rather than through group schemes. It serves the rigidification step for fake elliptic curves in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where kernels of $\mathcal O_D$-isogenies of formal modules have to be recognised as $\mathcal O_D$-stable subgroups.
